<main id="main">

  <!-- ============ MASTHEAD ============ -->
  <section class="masthead" aria-labelledby="project-title" style="padding-top:56px">
    <div class="wrap mast-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker mono">Research proposal · Quality of life · Disability · Built environment</p>
        <h1 id="project-title"><span class="scramble" data-text="ATLANTIS">ATLANTIS</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">.</span></h1>
        <p class="backronym">
          <b>A</b> <em>tactile</em>, <b>L</b>ived-experience <b>A</b>ssessment of <b>N</b>avigation,
          <b>T</b>echnology <b>A</b>nd <b>NT</b>… no — <b>In</b>clusive <b>S</b>paces.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          A 36-month, co-produced research program to find — <em>with and for</em> 48 disabled and
          disabled-adjacent residents — why some streets feel made for you and others made against
          you, and to turn that evidence into low-cost, open, adoptable fixes.
        </p>
        <dl class="meta reveal">
          <div><dt>Principal Investigator</dt><dd>Dr. Elena Vasquez</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Institution</dt><dd>Inst. for Inclusive Futures<small>Meridian University</small></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Duration</dt><dd>36 months</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Budget</dt><dd>$2,400,000</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Cohort</dt><dd>48 co-researchers</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Project ID</dt><dd class="mono" style="font-weight:600">atlantis-2025-047</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <a class="pill" href="#outcomes">Read the plain-language summary ↓</a>
      </div>
      <figure class="mast-fig reveal d2">
        <div class="frame kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/inclusive-street/860/1080" alt="A city street corner with tactile paving, wide crosswalk and a bench, illustrating the everyday built environment the project studies." width="860" height="1080"></div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 1 — Baseline environmental audit, constable district. (Illustrative)</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ TICKER ============ -->
  <div class="ticker" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="ticker-track">
      <div class="ticker-group">
        <span>Co-design <i>✦</i></span><span>Lived experience <i>✦</i></span><span>Universal design <i>✦</i></span>
        <span>WHOQOL-BREF <i>✦</i></span><span>WCAG 2.2 AA <i>✦</i></span><span>Plain language <i>✦</i></span>
        <span>Barrier Atlas <i>✦</i></span><span>Tactile paving <i>✦</i></span><span>Paid co-research <i>✦</i></span><span>Open toolkit <i>✦</i></span>
      </div>
      <div class="ticker-group">
        <span>Co-design <i>✦</i></span><span>Lived experience <i>✦</i></span><span>Universal design <i>✦</i></span>
        <span>WHOQOL-BREF <i>✦</i></span><span>WCAG 2.2 AA <i>✦</i></span><span>Plain language <i>✦</i></span>
        <span>Barrier Atlas <i>✦</i></span><span>Tactile paving <i>✦</i></span><span>Paid co-research <i>✦</i></span><span>Open toolkit <i>✦</i></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ 01 PROBLEM ============ -->
  <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-h">
    <div class="wrap problem-grid">
      <div class="sticky-col">
        <p class="sec"><b>01</b> The problem</p>
        <h2 id="problem-h" class="lm"><span>It is not a medical problem. It is an environmental one.</span></h2>
        <p class="big-ratio reveal">1<b>:</b>6</p>
        <p class="ratio-note reveal d1">people on earth lives with a significant disability — and most of their exclusion is designed, not destiny.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-copy">
        <p class="reveal">Ask a disabled person what lowers their quality of life and the answer rarely names a diagnosis. It names a <strong>curb</strong>. A <strong>door</strong>. A ticket kiosk at head height with no staff in sight. A bus stop that announces nothing. The barriers are ordinary, invisible to planners, and repeated ten thousand times a day.</p>
        <p class="reveal d1">ATLANTIS treats those barriers as the unit of study. Over 36 months we will measure quality of life with the validated WHOQOL-BREF instrument, map and score the everyday places that shape it, and test small, cheap, co-designed fixes — then measure again. Where a pilot works, it ships as an open toolkit and a one-page policy brief a city can copy next quarter.</p>
        <div class="stats" role="list" aria-label="Key statistics">
          <div class="stat" role="listitem"><b><span data-count="1.3" data-decimals="1">0</span><i>B</i></b><span>People with significant disability worldwide — ≈16% of the population (WHO)</span></div>
          <div class="stat" role="listitem"><b><span data-count="80">0</span><i>%</i></b><span>Live in low- and lower-middle-income countries, where access gaps are widest (WHO)</span></div>
          <div class="stat" role="listitem"><b><span data-count="65">0</span><i>%</i></b><span>Of adults with disabilities report serious difficulty accessing their own neighborhood (WHO)</span></div>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="quote reveal d2">
          “The distance between your home and your dignity is paved by decisions no one makes with you.”
          <cite>— Panel member, co-design session 3, pilot study</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 02 QUESTIONS ============ -->
  <section id="questions" class="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec"><b>02</b> Research questions</p>
      <h2 id="questions-h" class="lm" style="margin-bottom:44px"><span>Four questions, asked in the panel's words</span></h2>

      <div class="rq open">
        <h3><button class="rq-btn" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="rq1">
          <span class="rq-tag">RQ 1</span>
          <span>Which built-environment and service-system features most strongly predict quality of life among disabled residents across three urban contexts?</span>
          <span class="rq-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="rq-panel" id="rq1"><div class="rq-inner">
          <div class="rq-body">
            <span class="via">Tested via — WHOQOL-BREF baselines × 1,200+ scored POI audit, multilevel regression</span>
            The core predictive model. Neighborhoods are treated as a layer, not noise: we ask which features of a 500-m walking catchment carry weight — tactile paving continuity, seating density, transit announcement quality, kerb ramps, information formats — and where that weight differs between cities.
          </div>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq">
        <h3><button class="rq-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rq2">
          <span class="rq-tag">RQ 2</span>
          <span>Which low-cost environmental interventions, designed with the constable cohort, produce the largest measurable QoL gain per dollar spent?</span>
          <span class="rq-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="rq-panel" id="rq2"><div class="rq-inner">
          <div class="rq-body">
            <span class="via">Tested via — 12 pilots, pre/post QoL measurement, cost-per-point model</span>
            The project's practical engine. The panel shortlists candidate fixes under a strict budget ceiling; we instrument them, run pre/post QoL measurement, and publish a cost-per-QoL-point ranking that any city budget office can apply to its own backlog.
          </div>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq">
        <h3><button class="rq-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rq3">
          <span class="rq-tag">RQ 3</span>
          <span>How do assistive and information technologies — audio wayfinding, haptic alerts, transit APIs — reduce or compound environmental barriers in daily navigation?</span>
          <span class="rq-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="rq-panel" id="rq3"><div class="rq-inner">
          <div class="rq-body">
            <span class="via">Tested via — paired tech-on / tech-off route audits, diary studies (n = 48)</span>
            Technology is a variable, not a saviour. We pair route audits with and without specific technologies to isolate which tools genuinely open doors and which quietly shift the burden onto the user — new batteries, new interfaces, new ways to be excluded.
          </div>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq">
        <h3><button class="rq-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rq4">
          <span class="rq-tag">RQ 4</span>
          <span>Which policy and code levers — zoning, tactile-paving standards, procurement rules — turn local pilot wins into durable, system-level access?</span>
          <span class="rq-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="rq-panel" id="rq4"><div class="rq-inner">
          <div class="rq-body">
            <span class="via">Tested via — structured sessions with 3 municipal partners, policy-simulation workshops</span>
            The scale question. Pilot wins that die at the project boundary are wasted. Working with three municipal partners we trace each successful intervention back to the clause, code or procurement rule that can entrench it — and brief on only budget-neutral levers.
          </div>
        </div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 03 METHODS ============ -->
  <section id="methods" aria-labelledby="methods-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec"><b>03</b> Method</p>
      <h2 id="methods-h" class="lm" style="margin-bottom:52px"><span>Four phases, one standing panel</span></h2>

      <div class="stack">
        <article class="phase">
          <div class="phase-body">
            <div class="phase-top"><span class="phase-num">01</span><span class="phase-months">Months 1 – 8</span></div>
            <h3>Listening</h3>
            <p>Co-design workshops and narrative interviews (n = 48). The panel writes the audit protocol, the success criteria, and the panel charter before any measurement begins. Baseline WHOQOL-BREF completed for every member.</p>
            <div class="chips"><span>Panel charter</span><span>Baseline QoL dataset</span><span>Audit protocol v1</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="phase-img kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/phase-listening/560/420" alt="A co-design workshop in progress: participants gathered around a table covered in maps and notes." loading="lazy"></div>
        </article>

        <article class="phase">
          <div class="phase-body">
            <div class="phase-top"><span class="phase-num">02</span><span class="phase-months">Months 9 – 18</span></div>
            <h3>Auditing</h3>
            <p>Panel members walk, roll, and navigate their own neighborhoods with the open audit toolkit. 1,200+ points of interest are scored on six access dimensions. The data becomes a public <em>Barrier Atlas</em> for the three study cities.</p>
            <div class="chips"><span>Open audit toolkit v1</span><span>Barrier Atlas — 3 cities</span><span>1,200+ scored POIs</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="phase-img kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/phase-auditing/560/420" alt="Close view of tactile paving at a crosswalk, one of the features scored during the environmental audit." loading="lazy"></div>
        </article>

        <article class="phase">
          <div class="phase-body">
            <div class="phase-top"><span class="phase-num">03</span><span class="phase-months">Months 19 – 30</span></div>
            <h3>Piloting</h3>
            <p>Twelve co-designed, low-cost interventions run in situ: tactile paving patches, audio signage, seating, app-based wayfinding. Each is measured with pre/post QoL scores and unit cost, feeding the cost-per-QoL-point model.</p>
            <div class="chips"><span>12 pilots</span><span>Pre/post QoL measurement</span><span>Cost-per-point model</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="phase-img kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/phase-piloting/560/420" alt="Community members testing a new seating and signage installation on a public street." loading="lazy"></div>
        </article>

        <article class="phase">
          <div class="phase-body">
            <div class="phase-top"><span class="phase-num">04</span><span class="phase-months">Months 31 – 36</span></div>
            <h3>Scaling</h3>
            <p>Synthesis and hand-off. Toolkit v2 is released under an open licence, policy briefs go to the three municipal partners, and the evidence is published plain-language-first — the one-page summary is the lead document, not the appendix.</p>
            <div class="chips"><span>Policy briefs ×3</span><span>Toolkit v2 (CC-BY)</span><span>Plain-language evidence map</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="phase-img kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/phase-scaling/560/420" alt="An urban planning office wall covered with maps and pinned notes from the final synthesis phase." loading="lazy"></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 04 COHORT ============ -->
  <section id="cohort" aria-labelledby="cohort-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec"><b>04</b> Participants</p>
      <h2 id="cohort-h" class="lm"><span>The constable cohort</span></h2>
      <div class="cohort-grid">
        <div class="cohort-copy">
          <p class="reveal">Fieldwork is anchored in the <span class="hl">constable district</span> — a dense, mixed-income neighbourhood chosen not for its problems but for its people: 48 residents with mobility, sensory, cognitive, and chronic-health disabilities who serve as <strong>co-researchers, not subjects</strong>.</p>
          <p class="reveal d1">The panel meets biweekly for design sittings, owns the audit protocol, votes on which pilots get built, and holds a standing veto over how any member's story is used in outputs. Nothing about them, without them — that line from the Disability Rights movement is the operational spine of the project, not a slogan on the cover page.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:36px">
            <div class="principle reveal"><b>P1</b><div><h3>Lived expertise first</h3><p>The panel designs the questions, the tools, and the criteria of success before the academic team instruments anything.</p></div></div>
            <div class="principle reveal d1"><b>P2</b><div><h3>Pay knowledge work</h3><p>Co-researchers are paid at project-researcher rates. “Stipends” are a euphemism for exploitation and are banned from this budget.</p></div></div>
            <div class="principle reveal d2"><b>P3</b><div><h3>Access is co-authorship</h3><p>Every workshop, document, and venue is planned around the access needs the panel declares — captioning, seating, formats, pace.</p></div></div>
            <div class="principle reveal d3"><b>P4</b><div><h3>Plain language is the default</h3><p>The one-page summary is the lead publication in four languages; the 40-page technical report exists to support it, not the reverse.</p></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="cohort-fig reveal d2">
          <div class="frame kb"><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/constable-panel/900/680" alt="The constable district community panel meeting: a diverse group of people seated together in a community hall." loading="lazy"></div>
          <figcaption>Fig. 2 — Panel sitting 14, constable community hall. (Illustrative)</figcaption>
          <span class="badge mono">n = 48 · co-researchers</span>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 05 OUTCOMES ============ -->
  <section id="outcomes" class="dark" aria-labelledby="outcomes-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec" style="color:rgba(241,236,225,.6)"><b>05</b> Outcomes</p>
      <h2 id="outcomes-h" class="lm"><span>What the money buys, exactly</span></h2>

      <div class="metrics">
        <div class="metric"><b><span data-count="48">0</span></b><span>Paid co-researchers on the standing panel</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><b><span data-count="1200">0</span><i>+</i></b><span>Points of interest audited &amp; scored</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><b><span data-count="12">0</span></b><span>Low-cost pilots built &amp; evaluated</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><b><span data-count="3">0</span></b><span>City partners receiving open toolkits</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="deliver"><b>D-01</b><h3>Open Environmental Audit Toolkit v2</h3><p>CC-BY, WCAG 2.2 AA conformant, with a paper-only mode for low-connectivity and low-vision users.</p></div>
      <div class="deliver"><b>D-02</b><h3>The Barrier Atlas</h3><p>A living, open map of scored points of interest across the three study cities, maintained by the panel after the project ends.</p></div>
      <div class="deliver"><b>D-03</b><h3>Policy briefs × 3</h3><p>One per municipal partner. Budget-neutral levers only: code clauses, procurement rules, and maintenance priorities.</p></div>
      <div class="deliver"><b>D-04</b><h3>Longitudinal QoL dataset</h3><p>De-identified WHOQOL-BREF measures across 12 waves, open-licensed with the panel's governance protocol attached.</p></div>
      <div class="deliver"><b>D-05</b><h3>Plain-language evidence map</h3><p>The full findings in one visual page, in four languages, with an audio-narrated version — published first, everywhere.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 06 ETHICS ============ -->
  <section id="ethics" class="ethics" aria-labelledby="ethics-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec"><b>06</b> Ethics &amp; access</p>
      <h2 id="ethics-h" class="lm"><span>The proposal's own access standards</span></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Independent ethics approval, plus a panel veto over the use of any member's story, route data, or likeness.</li>
        <li>All digital deliverables meet WCAG 2.2 AA; print materials at 16 pt minimum, dyslexia-friendly layouts on request.</li>
        <li>Data minimisation throughout; anonymisation by default; consent re-verified at the start of every phase.</li>
        <li>Dedicated protocols for chronic-illness and cognitive-access members — including rest pauses, no-surprise scheduling, and exit without justification.</li>
        <li>Every session captioned live; transcripts delivered to the panel within 48 hours, at their level of detail.</li>
        <li>A community feedback loop precedes every publication: panel review, red-line comments, 14-day turnaround, no exceptions.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 07 BUDGET + TEAM ============ -->
  <section id="budget" class="budget" aria-labelledby="budget-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec"><b>07</b> Budget &amp; team</p>
      <h2 id="budget-h" class="lm"><span>$2.4M — most of it goes to people</span></h2>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption class="mono" style="caption-side:top;text-align:left;font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-soft);padding:14px 18px 0">Indicative budget — 36 months, USD</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col" class="amt">Amount</th><th scope="col" class="amt">Share</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Personnel — researchers <em>and</em> paid disability co-researchers</td><td class="amt">$980,000</td><td class="amt">40.8%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Participant &amp; co-researcher compensation</td><td class="amt">$410,000</td><td class="amt">17.1%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Intervention pilots — materials, installation, evaluation</td><td class="amt">$520,000</td><td class="amt">21.7%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Audit toolkit &amp; sensing hardware</td><td class="amt">$260,000</td><td class="amt">10.8%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dissemination, plain-language output, accessibility remediation</td><td class="amt">$90,000</td><td class="amt">3.8%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Contingency</td><td class="amt">$140,000</td><td class="amt">5.8%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
          <tfoot><tr><td>Total</td><td class="amt">$2,400,000</td><td class="amt">100.0%</td></tr></tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="sec" style="margin-bottom:28px"><b>·</b> Core team</p>
      <div class="team-grid">
